    summary: "Opening with the weather, as in it was a dark and stormy night, a grey morning or the sun rose over the hills, is among the best-known habits of beginners and a source of parody. Weather can still open a book well when it does work: it sets a problem, a mood the story needs, or a character's attention.",
    conv: ["Weather openers often come before anything is happening, which delays the story.", "The opener works when weather is a pressure on the characters, such as a storm that traps them.", "Weather that matches mood, such as rain for sadness, is a familiar device that readers see coming.", "A specific weather detail seen through a character's attention can tell more than general description.", "Contrast can work better than match: a bright day at a grim event.", "Weather is also a frame device in many genres, such as gothic and noir, with conventions of its own."],
