    name: "The mirror description",
    summary: "A character who looks in a mirror to give the reader their eye colour, hair and height is a famous shortcut that many editors call a cliché. The device can work when the mirror scene is itself about something, such as a changed face or a disguise, and not only a hook for description.",
    conv: ["The usual pattern is a character examining themself in a mirror, window or puddle and listing features.", "People rarely list their own features while looking in a mirror, so the moment can feel staged.", "It works when the character has a reason to look: after an injury, before a performance, or in a new body.", "Other ways to deliver description include what others notice, how the character handles their own appearance, and details that arrive when they matter.", "A first-person narrator has a particular problem, since people seldom describe themselves, and writers solve it in different ways.", "Not every novel needs a full physical description at all."],
