    kind: "usage",
    name: "Clichés, stock phrases and dead metaphors",
    summary: "A cliché is a phrase or image worn smooth by use, such as cold as ice, quiet as a mouse or at the end of the day. Many are dead metaphors that readers no longer see as images. The usual advice is to notice them and decide: renew the image, say the thing plainly, or keep the phrase on purpose, as in a character's speech.",
    conv: ["Families of cliché include similes (cold as ice), sayings and idioms (at the end of the day, needle in a haystack), scene types (the mirror, the weather opening) and body reactions.", "Dead metaphors, such as the leg of a table, are not a problem; the issue is stock images that stand in for observation.", "Renewing an image means making it specific to this character, place and moment.", "A plain statement often beats a worn image.", "Characters may speak in clichés, which can reveal temperament, class or a habit of avoiding thought.", "Cliché is relative: what is stock for one audience, genre or era is fresh to another."],
