    summary: "A light-verb phrase pairs a general verb such as make, take, give or have with a noun that carries the real action: make a decision, take a look, give an answer. They are normal English and natural in speech, but in tight prose a single verb, such as decide, look or answer, can say the same with less.",
    conv: ["Typical patterns include make a decision, take a walk, give an answer, have a conversation, carry out an investigation and perform an analysis.", "The single-verb versions are decide, walk, answer, talk, investigate and analyse.", "These phrases also let writers vary tone: take a look is casual, while examine is formal.", "In speech they are common and natural; in formal writing, strings of them pile up into bureaucratic prose.", "Some light-verb phrases add meaning that the plain verb lacks: take a shower, have a laugh and give a sigh describe an event with a shape.", "When such nouns pile up in a sentence, the prose can sound bureaucratic."],
